      "id": "pixel-party",
      "type": "project",
      "title": "Pixel Party",
      "kicker": "Real-time multiplayer drawing game",
      "year": "2025",
      "summary": "A four-person build of a real-time drawing-and-guessing game. Players draw on a shared canvas while the lobby guesses in chat — every stroke, guess and timer tick travels over WebSockets and lands in MongoDB.",
      "body": "\n            <h2>Pixel Party</h2>\n            <p>\n              4-man project to showcase our ability to use React, CSS, Node.js, Express, and MongoDB\n              Atlas\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_1.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party lobby with the shared drawing canvas\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Weeks 1-2</h3>\n            <p>\n              The goal was to making a functional multiplayer game where a player can draw an image\n              on the screen, and the other members of the lobby guess the word in the chatbox. We\n              spent sprint 1 setting up the login/register rest api calls.\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_2.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party word-selection screen for the drawer\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Weeks 3-4</h3>\n            <p>\n              At the core of the game, we wanted some variables in the game that we could control.\n              We started developing a logo and background theme. Our main inspiration was skribbl.io\n              so we tried to mimic that game as much as possible.\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_3.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party drawing round in progress\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Weeks 5-6</h3>\n            <p>\n              All aspects of the game including chat, drawing, and word-selection use the WebSocket\n              package on the frontend and backend. This is essentially two singletons playing ping\n              pong, both waiting for keywords to callback functions for the server/client to\n              process. This happends in realtime, sending out a mass-message to all players in the\n              lobby.\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_4.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party chat and guessing interface\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Week 5-6</h3>\n            <p>\n              Once the game starts, the server is essentially running \"on-rails\" with a timer. The\n              client selects a word, they start drawing, other players can see said drawing and\n              guess in the chat. Obviously, the word is not shared until the end. Guessing correctly\n              rewards the drawer and the person guesssing. If time runs out we move onto the next\n              drawer.\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_5.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party scoreboard between rounds\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Weeks 7-8</h3>\n            <p>\n              The round restarts with a new drawer. The letters above the drawing can slowly be\n              revealed at the game-leader's chosen speed. Once everyone has drawn once, we calculate\n              the score based on who guess right the fastest, and who drew correctly enough to\n              guess. A winner is decided, or the second round starts.\n            </p>\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_6.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party game interface detail\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_7.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party alternate layout\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n\n            <img src=\"/img/p2_8.webp\" alt=\"Pixel Party final round\" style=\"width: 100%; border-radius: var(--border-radius); margin-bottom: 1.5rem\">\n            <h3>Conclusion</h3>\n            <p>\n              Our biggest hurdles were getting the websockets to sync across devices. This included\n              getting the drawing aspect to work, as well as running the whole show on a timer that\n              could allow users to join the game at any time. Not only are we transmitting data in\n              realtime we're also saving it to the database, so we have be prepared to load that at\n              any time should someone disconnect.\n            </p>\n          ",
