      "id": "javascript-async-patterns",
      "type": "post",
      "title": "JavaScript Async Patterns",
      "kicker": "Writing",
      "date": "2026-03-19",
      "summary": "A comprehensive guide to JavaScript asynchronous patterns including callbacks, promises, and async/await.",
      "body": "\n          <p>\n            In this post, we will explore various asynchronous patterns in JavaScript, including\n            callbacks, promises, and async/await.\n          </p>\n\n          <h2>Callbacks</h2>\n          <p>\n            Callbacks are functions passed as arguments to other functions. They are executed after\n            the completion of a task.\n          </p>\n\n          <pre><code>// Example of a callback\nfunction fetchData(callback) {\n    setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n        const data = { id: 1, name: 'John Doe' };\n        callback(data);\n    }, 1000);\n}\n\n// Example of using fetchData\nfetchData((data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('Fetched data:', data);\n});\n</code></pre>\n\n          <p>\n            While callbacks work well for simple operations, they can lead to \"callback hell\" when\n            dealing with multiple asynchronous operations.\n          </p>\n\n          <h2>Promises</h2>\n          <p>\n            Promises provide a more elegant way to handle asynchronous operations. A promise\n            represents a value that may not be available yet.\n          </p>\n\n          <pre><code>// Converting the callback example to a promise\nfunction fetchDataPromise() {\n    return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {\n        setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n            const data = { id: 1, name: 'John Doe' };\n            resolve(data);\n            // If there was an error, we could call: reject(error)\n        }, 1000);\n    });\n}\n\n// Using the promise\nfetchDataPromise()\n    .then(data =&gt; {\n        console.log('Fetched data:', data);\n        return processData(data);\n    })\n    .then(processedData =&gt; {\n        console.log('Processed data:', processedData);\n    })\n    .catch(error =&gt; {\n        console.error('Error:', error);\n    });\n\nfunction processData(data) {\n    return { ...data, processed: true };\n}</code></pre>\n\n          <p>\n            Promises provide better error handling with the <code>.catch()</code> method and allow\n            for chaining multiple asynchronous operations.\n          </p>\n\n          <h2>Async/Await</h2>\n          <p>\n            Async/await is syntactic sugar over promises, making asynchronous code look and behave\n            more like synchronous code.\n          </p>\n\n          <pre><code>// Using async/await with our fetchDataPromise function\nasync function fetchAndProcessData() {\n    try {\n        const data = await fetchDataPromise();\n        console.log('Fetched data:', data);\n        \n        const processedData = await processData(data);\n        console.log('Processed data:', processedData);\n        \n        return processedData;\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error('Error:', error);\n    }\n}\n\n// Call the async function\nfetchAndProcessData().then(result =&gt; {\n    console.log('Final result:', result);\n});</code></pre>\n\n          <p>\n            Async/await makes the code more readable and maintainable, especially for complex\n            asynchronous operations. The <code>await</code> keyword can only be used inside an\n            <code>async</code> function.\n          </p>\n\n          <h2>Practical Comparison</h2>\n          <p>Let's compare these patterns with a practical example of fetching data from an API:</p>\n\n          <h3>Using Callbacks</h3>\n          <pre><code>function getUser(userId, callback) {\n    const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();\n    xhr.open('GET', `https://api.example.com/users/${userId}`);\n    xhr.onload = function() {\n        if (xhr.status === 200) {\n            callback(null, JSON.parse(xhr.responseText));\n        } else {\n            callback(new Error(`Request failed: ${xhr.status}`));\n        }\n    };\n    xhr.onerror = function() {\n        callback(new Error('Network error'));\n    };\n    xhr.send();\n}</code></pre>\n\n          <h3>Using Promises</h3>\n          <pre><code>function getUser(userId) {\n    return fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${userId}`)\n        .then(response =&gt; {\n            if (!response.ok) {\n                throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);\n            }\n            return response.json();\n        });\n}</code></pre>\n\n          <h3>Using Async/Await</h3>\n          <pre><code>async function getUser(userId) {\n    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${userId}`);\n    \n    if (!response.ok) {\n        throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);\n    }\n    \n    return response.json();\n}</code></pre>\n\n          <h2>Conclusion</h2>\n          <p>Each asynchronous pattern in JavaScript has its use cases:</p>\n\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Callbacks:</strong> Simple operations and working with older APIs</li>\n            <li>\n              <strong>Promises:</strong> Improved error handling and chaining multiple operations\n            </li>\n            <li>\n              <strong>Async/Await:</strong> Most readable approach for complex asynchronous logic\n            </li>\n          </ul>\n\n          <p>\n            Modern JavaScript development leans heavily toward Promises and Async/Await for their\n            readability and maintainability. When dealing with asynchronous code, choose the pattern\n            that makes your code most understandable to yourself and others.\n          </p>\n        ",
      "tags": [
        "JavaScript",
        "Async",
        "Patterns"
      ],
      "href": "/blog/javascript-async-patterns.html",
      "linkLabel": "Read the article",
      "number": 3
